#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184074 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184175 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d702975-166e-5067-ae3c-1152fade8248" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace"}] :question/id #uuid "edd4d491-c483-5004-b30f-68cf0ef76f43" :question/math-academy-id "e-5922" :question/problem "Find the distance between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ where\n![](images/51/5154eac29b8dee2a4f1ae4212306f80d5ff7975d38751a222ef2027d35acc234.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S} \\: \\mathbf{x} = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T\\,\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n![](images/ac/acee6cac9fd5dec811783141ecdcfd07402d6482146cdbdcac8f6e536416f0b7.svg)\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 18\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{4}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3-(-3))^{2}+(5-5)^{2}+(-1-1)^{2}+(3-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+0+4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184075 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184176 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26b41192-9680-55c3-930c-52ff235e1800" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto the Solution Space of a Linear System"}] :question/id #uuid "04cb9ecc-68fa-546e-aa96-8a1ee050bbed" :question/math-academy-id "e-5923" :question/problem "Given the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the solution space of the system.\n![](images/5a/5ad675762a16e4c81d5d83161dc24646121e4ca10b1e8a4957d7e9a317fc1fc4.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis for the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2,$ and $x_3$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get $x_1 = 2x_2-3x_3.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n![](images/fe/fe0dd0d87977a4b16d56a96e54a5db6b56bd7cb9f5182a9c43ff6e14f03d2c7a.svg)\n\nConsequently, \n\n![](images/93/9335bf579d464c3e54a92459199d585c8e42b5ef21ee9382436439d050338c9e.svg)\nis a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we let $A$ be a matrix whose columns equal the elements of $\\mathcal B,$ and we find the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the solution space of the system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}5 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{14}[\\begin{aligned}10 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184111 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184207 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1224e2dd-96bc-5c24-aacf-3c36a7c3a1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acute Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace"}] :question/id #uuid "98aed663-0f72-5494-9a5f-ba2fdf94764a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9906" :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\},$ where\n![](images/13/13b2b59a69e2fd1562f9b835128a16332c7fbd32646f662efe7dbf06bf5cac56.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S} \\: \\mathbf{x} = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T\\,\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n![](images/bb/bb65806cb07e71dc92a5c55c210df1112da1f2500ed6cf18ef2a373d155aea94.svg)\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}27 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 27\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{72}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf{x}.$ Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to $\\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf{x}.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n![](images/54/54a3c6bd24d0c021c0aa65ca1f12c6470b83744c46f984bdb076287b076fc9c1.svg)\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{(-1)}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos\\left( \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}2\\right)= \\dfrac\\pi 4,\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle, as desired."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416324 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184175 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d702975-166e-5067-ae3c-1152fade8248" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6042ceb6-39dd-5861-ab98-2ba38c0dfad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416313} {:answer/id #uuid "6b6a5f2f-76cf-57a5-b55b-09e192c66fac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416314} {:answer/id #uuid "c6a4b7c1-b28b-59e9-b2e0-44d2e77e5df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416315} {:answer/id #uuid "db2fadc7-9954-5d2d-9567-580bac75c277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416316} {:answer/id #uuid "ff56f552-cb5d-52ad-a5a1-61418ef3f448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db2fadc7-9954-5d2d-9567-580bac75c277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416316} :answer-field/id #uuid "db7e3155-6118-5747-b4e5-472254e85f83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d0d68cfe-73a4-5cae-a88d-ce4c5ac5ed06" :question/math-academy-id "q-139469" :question/problem "Find the distance between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{90}[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{4}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1-2)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}+(-3-(-1))^{2}+(-3-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+0+4+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416323 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184175 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d702975-166e-5067-ae3c-1152fade8248" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f092c3d-8556-5f6a-af54-6cf54502714d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416311} {:answer/id #uuid "1a899cfc-2b1e-5341-b7ff-75e82ccc677e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416312} {:answer/id #uuid "ee402ce0-013b-55b8-989b-cc8ea6267cb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416317} {:answer/id #uuid "eef9c29f-2ba3-5765-9533-90dd311f676f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416318} {:answer/id #uuid "facc80f1-1b35-55ea-977b-cfdffdd16d9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a899cfc-2b1e-5341-b7ff-75e82ccc677e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416312} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba894365-a618-5f73-93d7-cf99f582f802" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0bd3b27-d4c9-5213-b407-098030561a8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-139473" :question/problem "Find the distance between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{16}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-3)^{2}+(6-1)^{2}+(4-(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{50} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184779 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184207 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1224e2dd-96bc-5c24-aacf-3c36a7c3a1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acute Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "196d5cce-e267-5d34-a516-34681744c454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370908} {:answer/id #uuid "258223e1-67f4-5d69-9403-e0dfa71d0741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370909} {:answer/id #uuid "46bfc1f1-7af8-5456-b790-9518a6d1e104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370910} {:answer/id #uuid "9b43d77c-a34b-511a-997b-b60ed018b565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370911} {:answer/id #uuid "624c5226-5398-5c9f-9b82-bb93208666e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46bfc1f1-7af8-5456-b790-9518a6d1e104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370910} :answer-field/id #uuid "664cad5a-9d78-59a1-8bc8-98ba011fc35d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6fed417-1aa3-54f6-b260-a0764f066874" :question/math-academy-id "q-40578" :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $x$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,2\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6}\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326865 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184176 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26b41192-9680-55c3-930c-52ff235e1800" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto the Solution Space of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ff68ced-affe-5cf1-a209-321681e9cf42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324118} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa1b751-24d3-5fa8-9dca-424df4008b99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324481} {:answer/id #uuid "5e34c046-3427-5dd1-8f5c-9c9e21ea754f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324753} {:answer/id #uuid "c184f260-c54c-59a4-8e0e-833ee5bd8bac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325635} {:answer/id #uuid "f156c4c0-9394-528f-8104-3954bd7c2c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f156c4c0-9394-528f-8104-3954bd7c2c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326099} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba4e1a40-c1ef-5234-8ebc-e44a10f57fd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c9d8685d-2896-58f6-9f97-cb8ee658ca47" :question/math-academy-id "q-40619" :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis for the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184176 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26b41192-9680-55c3-930c-52ff235e1800" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto the Solution Space of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15dd4b81-6252-5a69-bca0-e1fd124cd255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394170} {:answer/id #uuid "b85664a1-3fab-583f-971d-9811e99660ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394171} {:answer/id #uuid "11f43327-ed18-5f77-aef7-7e1056596fc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394172} {:answer/id #uuid "6d037ede-ea38-51f8-bb04-853c04cd4083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394173} {:answer/id #uuid "3446844c-8f45-5cf2-8a13-bc819873c75d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3446844c-8f45-5cf2-8a13-bc819873c75d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394174} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f57172d-5f9c-5680-8423-f0e6ea5d7ffd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76b489c8-5c8f-576c-ba37-1623ccf3438d" :question/math-academy-id "q-81435" :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-4{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to its row echelon form. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184207 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1224e2dd-96bc-5c24-aacf-3c36a7c3a1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acute Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0321347d-f889-5169-a954-ad53ad6e3f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{32.54}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351592} {:answer/id #uuid "69ebb229-ddaa-5394-b197-c67dc5a728a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{37}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352506} {:answer/id #uuid "865713b7-36a4-53bf-bb66-1513a3d90bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{21}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352735} {:answer/id #uuid "c9d0d3b7-943b-501d-9dda-94bed23beb55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{19.47}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353323} {:answer/id #uuid "dad94daf-b403-53d4-9765-1b9e1321c6bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45.77}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9d0d3b7-943b-501d-9dda-94bed23beb55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{19.47}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353323} :answer-field/id #uuid "3828b66d-f82a-5813-9ed6-8d2fc865ed33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "520b11ad-97ee-56c3-9607-d0a21f040e1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-86829" :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRound the answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $x$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,3\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot (-4)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{3}\\sqrt{24}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{3}\\sqrt{3\\cdot 8}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{8}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{8}}{3})≈{19.47}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."}]}
